ntdll: restore GSBASE after redirecting a direct syscall on macOS.

Programs that issue the syscall instruction themselves (for example the
copy protection of some games) trap into sigsys_handler(), which sends them
to __wine_syscall_dispatcher_prolog_end. On macOS, init_handler() switched
GSBASE to the pthread TSD, and returning from the signal does not switch it
back. The dispatcher then loads the TEB from %gs:0x30, gets a TSD slot
instead, and faults when it reads amd64_thread_data()->pthread_teb (address
0x320), so every direct syscall fails with STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION.

Point GSBASE back at the TEB before returning, as leave_handler() does for
other signals.

For Wine 11.21 with Wine Staging 11.21 applied. LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -2608,6 +2608,12 @@
         frame->restore_flags |= CONTEXT_CONTROL;
     }
     RIP_sig(sigcontext) = (ULONG64)__wine_syscall_dispatcher_prolog_end_ptr;
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    /* init_handler() pointed GSBASE at the pthread TSD, and the signal return
+     * does not restore it. The dispatcher continues as if entered from PE
+     * code and reads the TEB through %gs, so point GSBASE back at the TEB. */
+    _thread_set_tsd_base( (uint64_t)data->teb );
+#endif
 }
 #endif
 
